1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,500
This is eProcurement: one tenant, one procure-to-pay story, end to end.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,300
Everything runs locally on Docker Compose, seeded by make demo.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:14,400
We sign in as the requester and pin the UI to English.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,700
The locale is a cookie — it survives navigation for the whole demo.

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:26,200
New requisition: a four-step wizard — details, items, attachments, review.

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:33,000
Currency IDR. One line — two laptops; the price field is cents: seven and a half million.

7
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:38,300
The basket autosaves as a draft — the badge confirms it.

8
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:46,200
Attachments go straight to MinIO on a presigned URL — and only while the requisition is still a draft.

9
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:52,300
Review shows the total — fifteen million cents, one hundred fifty thousand rupiah.

10
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:58,200
Submit. PR-000001, status submitted.

11
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:06,500
One honesty note: the vendor came pre-created by make demo — the vendors page is read-only, no create form yet.

12
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,800
PT Demo Makmur, active — the vendor this story uses.

13
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:16,200
Switch to the approver — self-approval is forbidden, even for admins.

14
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:21,700
The approval panel appears. Approve, with a note.

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,800
Status flips to approved; the step records the reason.

16
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:39,700
The timeline is the MongoDB event history — submitted, step approved, approved — each decision lands within seconds.

17
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:46,400
Now the buyer leg. Convert-to-PO has no UI yet — issue one-twelve — so it's one API call.

18
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:53,900
Three commands: log in, look up the vendor, create the PO from the requisition.

19
00:01:54,200 --> 00:02:00,000
The answer: PO-000001. In the UI, the requisition is closed.

20
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:06,000
Issue the purchase order — issued.

21
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:11,700
Acknowledge the vendor — acknowledged.

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:24,600
Convert, issue and acknowledge all wrote events through the same transactional outbox — remember that for the resilience chapter.

23
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:31,000
Record a partial goods receipt. The line shows two remaining — receive one.

24
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:39,300
GRN-000001: two ordered, one received, one remaining — partially received, deliberately not received.

25
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:46,800
Now attempt the over-receipt: two, against a remaining of one.

26
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:53,600
The form refuses, inline — nothing is submitted.

27
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:59,800
The server holds the same guard as a typed backstop: 409, grn_over_receipt.

28
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:06,400
Finance. Create the invoice on the partial receipt — bill what was received.

29
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:12,500
INV-DEMO-001, quantity one, at the PO price — pending.

30
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:20,300
Upload the vendor invoice document. Until an attachment is ready, Run match stays disabled — the hard gate.

31
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:25,200
Run match — verdict: Matched.

32
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:34,500
The line reads: ordered two, received one, invoiced one — deltas zero. The match compares invoiced against received, not ordered.

33
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:44,600
The tolerances — five percent quantity, two percent price — are per-tenant config; today that's an API call.

34
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:52,200
Dashboard. Thirty-ninety-day spend: one hundred fifty thousand rupiah — the issued PO total.

35
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:58,500
Spend by vendor: PT Demo Makmur. PO status: partially received, one.

36
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,800
Reports. The export history starts empty — every demo reset begins here.

37
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:10,700
Run export now — a manual run succeeds, one row.

38
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,700
In MinIO the CSV lands under exports, tenant, today — a clock-stamped filename.

39
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,600
One data row: PO-000001, PT Demo Makmur, one hundred fifty thousand rupiah.

40
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:29,700
Resilience. Stop the worker.

41
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,500
Trigger the export once, from the API: 202, requested. The run history does not move.

42
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:42,300
Nothing is lost — it is waiting in the outbox.

43
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:48,400
Start the worker — the backlog drains; the queued run appears exactly once.

44
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:54,800
Consumers are idempotent, keyed on event id. No duplicates.

45
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:01,400
Tracing. In Jaeger: the gateway service, the purchase-order-issue operation.

46
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:09,400
One trace across gateway, procurement, the outbox publish, RabbitMQ, the worker, and the documents projection.

47
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:14,800
Every hop a span — the same request you just watched.

48
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:20,400
The same system on Grafana's eProcurement overview.

49
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:28,700
Outbox lag read the backlog's age when the worker restarted, then returned to zero as it drained.

50
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,600
DLQ depth: where a poison message would surface. That's the demo — thanks for watching.
